  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nFor the most part, yesterday into early this morning has turned into \npart 2 of a severe weather bust as supercell convection as skirted \naround the CWA to the west/southwest and east, mainly avoiding the \nwestern and central U.P. over the past 24 hours. That being said, \nsynoptic-scale severe weather ingredients still for the most part \nremain in place as MUCAPEs of greater than 1000 J/kg either remain \nacross the area or are replenished back up to that amount by the \nafternoon hours (if we receive convection overhead this morning). \nMeanwhile, bulk shear values still look to range from 30 to 65 \nknots, with an incoming Clipper low providing a forcing mechanism. \nWhile guidance has somewhat converged on a solution bringing at \nleast some showers and storms to the area today, I remain HIGHLY \nSKEPTICAL on the severe weather threat considering 1) the past two \ndays have been severe weather busts, either missing us to the \nwest/southwest or east and 2) the latest WOFS runs show most of the \nresolved updraft helicities > 70 m2/s2 missing our area entirely to \nour south and west. After having a call with SPC this morning, it \nstill seems probable that the far west could be grazed by some \npotentially severe convection; therefore a Severe Thunderstorm Watch \nis in effect until 14z this morning (or until cancelled) for Gogebic \nand Ontonagon counties. While the conditions for an isolated tornado \nno longer seem to be there, there is still a theoretical non-zero \nchance for some damaging hail and winds today. While showers and \nstorms are possible throughout the entirety of today, the best \nchances appear to occur during the late afternoon hours as shown by \nthe NBM wanting to bring likely (55-74%) to definite (75+%) chances \nof precipitation overhead during that time. As we approach the \nevening hours, expect the convection as well as the severe weather \nthreat to vanish as the Clipper pulls the showers and storms further \nsoutheast into Lower Michigan. \n\nExpect quieter weather as we move into the rest of the week as \nheights rise over the central CONUS, creating a quasi omega block \npattern. While cold air advection moving in tonight could create \nsome very light upslope drizzle showers in the north wind belts, \nthinking that we will remain dry due to sfc to mid-level ridging \nincreasing over the Upper Great Lakes. Thus, while we should see \ntemperatures closer to normal today and Tuesday, as the shortwave \nridge begins to depart on Wednesday, expect temperatures to climb to \nabove normal once again as we head towards the latter half of the \nweek. In addition, with the departure of the shortwave ridge, we \ncould see Northern Rockies/Canadian shortwaves move into the Upper \nMidwest near the end of this week into this weekend, allowing \nadditional showers and storms to return.",
